  "subtitle": "The $93,000 laserdisc editing machine that invented the timeline — the missing link between the flatbed and Final Cut",
  "why_scout_cares": "The first nonlinear video editing system, with a physical controller (KEM-style shuttle knob + trackball + relegendable LED soft keys) driving a touchscreen timeline over racks of industrial laserdisc players. 'It could almost be Final Cut Pro in 2005.' Only 24 units, $93,000, sold to Avid in 1993. DEFERRED: zero freely-licensed images exist (only © Lucasfilm blog and auction photos).",
